# deploy/Caddyfile -- Step 7.3 prep (ops track). The single reverse proxy
# docs/SPEC.md §14 names ("proxy": Caddy or Nginx -- Caddy chosen, kickoff
# decision #5, D-113-hosting-provider-agnostic/D-113-domains-softwareske).
#
# Every hostname is read from the environment via Caddy's own {$VAR}
# substitution -- never a literal hostname anywhere in this file (kickoff
# decision #6: "hostnames always from Settings/Caddyfile, never
# hardcoded"). The three names below are read from the SAME root .env file
# every other Compose service already reads (see .env.example):
#   DASHBOARD_HOSTNAME  e.g. app.softwareske.com
#   API_HOSTNAME        e.g. api.softwareske.com
#   WIDGET_HOSTNAME     e.g. widget.softwareske.com
#   CADDY_ACME_EMAIL    operator contact for Let's Encrypt
#
# Request body limits reuse UPLOAD_MAX_SIZE_BYTES -- the SAME env var
# backend Settings.upload_max_size_bytes already reads (field name
# upper-cased, confirmed against app/config.py) -- not a second,
# Caddy-only variable: one source of truth for the upload cap, not two
# numbers that can drift apart. The `:20971520` fallback in each
# `max_size` line below is Caddy's own placeholder-default syntax
# (verified live: `caddy validate` resolves it correctly whether or not
# the env var is set) and must match Settings.upload_max_size_bytes's
# own literal default (20 * 1024 * 1024) -- update both together if
# either changes.
#
# Automatic TLS: every site address below is a real hostname (not an IP or
# ":port"), which is what makes Caddy provision and renew certificates via
# Let's Encrypt on its own -- no certbot, no manual certificate handling.
#
# Phoenix is NEVER routed here, by construction: no site block below
# references the phoenix service, and none ever should (phoenix-data/
# PHOENIX_COLLECTOR_ENDPOINT already bind it to 127.0.0.1-only inside the
# Compose network -- deploy/docker-compose.yml's own comment). An operator
# reaches it only via an SSH tunnel (docs/deploy/RUNBOOK.md).
#
# [SECURITY] L1 PART C (#7) (out-of-band Phase 7 batch, 2026-10-17): the
# forwarded-client-IP trust boundary and Host validation this file's own
# previous version named as "not built here" are now both built --
# app/client_ip.py (the api service's own one IP-derivation helper) and
# app/main.py's TrustedHostMiddleware respectively.
#
# Forwarded-client-IP: deliberately NO `trusted_proxies` global option is
# set below. Confirmed against Caddy 2.10.2's own docs (the pinned
# `caddy:2.10.2` image above) -- the reverse_proxy directive page and the
# global `servers > trusted_proxies` option page -- that:
#   - with no `trusted_proxies` configured, Caddy trusts NO peer, and its
#     own forwarded-header behavior does not depend on this setting at
#     all: reverse_proxy "ignore[s] [X-Forwarded-For's] values from
#     incoming requests, to prevent spoofing" and sets a fresh header
#     instead -- unconditionally, for every request, trusted or not.
#   - `trusted_proxies` instead controls something this deployment does
#     not need: whether CADDY ITSELF treats an incoming request's own
#     X-Forwarded-For as authoritative for ITS OWN features (access
#     logs, the `client_ip` placeholder/matcher). This architecture has
#     no CDN or other proxy in front of Caddy (docs/SPEC.md §14 names
#     Caddy as THE proxy) -- Caddy is always the first hop a real
#     visitor's TCP connection reaches, so there is no "more upstream"
#     peer whose claimed IP Caddy would ever need to trust. Leaving
#     `trusted_proxies` at its secure, empty default is therefore the
#     CORRECT choice, not a gap.
#   - net effect: every X-Forwarded-For Caddy forwards to `api:8000`
#     contains exactly one value -- the real TCP peer Caddy itself
#     accepted the connection from -- regardless of what a visitor's own
#     browser/script claims. app/client_ip.py still parses defensively
#     (right-most entry only, never raises on a malformed/multi-valued
#     header) so this stays correct even if a future CDN is added in
#     front of Caddy with its own `trusted_proxies`.
#
# Host validation: every site block below is its own exact hostname.
# Unlike an earlier version of this file, that is NOT the whole story --
# a request matching none of the 3 is caught by the explicit `http://
# { abort }` catch-all near the end of this file (added by the SAME
# advisor-review follow-up named below, after finding live that Caddy's
# own un-matched-route default on an unmanaged host is a bare `200 OK`,
# not a rejection). app/main.py's TrustedHostMiddleware (api service,
# production only) is the SECOND, defence-in-depth layer, for any
# request that reaches that container directly -- and it allows BOTH
# api_hostname and dashboard_hostname (never just one): a real
# production-breaking bug, found by advisor review and confirmed live
# (2026-10-17), was api_hostname alone there, which 400'd every
# dashboard SPA `/api/*` call -- Caddy's own reverse_proxy passes the
# dashboard site's OWN Host header through unchanged on every one of
# them (see the dashboard site block's own request_body/handle lines
# below), never api_hostname's. A THIRD, narrower layer binds specific
# ROUTE FAMILIES to the correct hostname (app/host_binding.py,
# app/main.py's own `include_router(..., dependencies=[...])` calls) --
# dashboard/admin routes accepted only on dashboard_hostname,
# widget-public routes only on api_hostname -- so a request that
# reaches the api container directly cannot use EITHER allowed hostname
# for ANY route, only the one Caddy itself would actually have routed
# it under.
{
	email {$CADDY_ACME_EMAIL}
}

# Shared by every site block below -- one definition, no duplication
# (rule 9) across the dashboard/api/widget sites. frame-ancestors 'none'
# is common to all three -- EXCEPT Content-Security-Policy, which is
# deliberately NOT set here (each site block below sets its own CSP
# directly instead; api/widget both still end up with the identical
# bare "frame-ancestors 'none'" value this snippet used to carry).
#
# [SECURITY] ops/deploy/smoke-prod-stack.sh follow-up (2026-10-17): a
# REAL, previously-undetected bug, found live while building that
# script's own CSP check -- putting Content-Security-Policy in this
# snippet, with the dashboard site block ALSO setting its own, stricter
# CSP "to replace it", does NOT actually replace it. Confirmed via
# `caddy adapt`: Caddy compiles the snippet's own `header {...}` block
# (which runs before the dashboard's `handle /api/*`/`handle /assets/*`/
# `handle {}` blocks in the route chain) as `"deferred": true` -- applied
# as the LAST step before the response is sent, specifically so a
# response-generating handler further down the chain (reverse_proxy,
# file_server) cannot accidentally clobber it. The dashboard's own later,
# non-deferred `header Content-Security-Policy "..."` directive applies
# EARLIER and was the one being overwritten, backwards from this file's
# own prior comment's assumption ("Caddy's header directive overwrites a
# same-named header set earlier in the same site block" -- true for two
# ORDINARY, non-deferred directives, but not once one of them is
# deferred). The real, live symptom: every dashboard response carried
# the BARE "frame-ancestors 'none'" policy, never the strict one --
# `dashboard/e2e/csp-server.mjs` (Step 6.1's own Playwright CSP test)
# never caught this because it is a standalone Node static-file server
# that sets the strict CSP directly in JavaScript, and never exercises
# this real Caddyfile at all. Fixed the only fully reliable way: no two
# directives ever compete for the same header name in the same site
# block -- each site sets Content-Security-Policy exactly once, with
# its own final value, nothing to defer against.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Permissions-Policy "geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=(), usb=()"
		# Caddy's own identifying header, same "don't advertise the stack"
		# discipline backend/smoke-image.sh already checks for the api
		# service's own /health response.
		-Server
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dashboard: serves the built SPA and routes /api to the API on the SAME
# origin (dashboard_hostname), so the dashboard/admin surface needs no CORS
# middleware at all (D-6.1-cors-same-origin) -- confirmed, not guessed:
# the same path-preserving proxy shape as dashboard/e2e/csp-server.mjs's
# own /api/* proxy, which the dashboard's own e2e suite already runs
# against.
{$DASHBOARD_HOSTNAME} {
	import security_headers
	# Full strict CSP, byte-for-byte the same policy dashboard/e2e/
	# csp-server.mjs already proves the dashboard runs under (Step 6.1's
	# own live Playwright test) -- the ONLY Content-Security-Policy
	# directive in this site block (see security_headers's own updated
	# header comment above for why that snippet no longer sets one).
	header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'"

	request_body {
		max_size {$UPLOAD_MAX_SIZE_BYTES:20971520}
	}

	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}

	# Vite's own content-hashed build output (dashboard/dist/assets/*) --
	# the filename changes whenever the content does, so a long,
	# immutable cache is safe and correct.
	handle /assets/* {
		root * /srv/dashboard
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
		file_server
	}

	# Everything else: the SPA shell. Never cached (it is the one file a
	# redeploy changes without a new filename), and any unmatched path
	# falls back to it so client-side routing works on a hard refresh.
	handle {
		root * /srv/dashboard
		header Cache-Control "no-cache"
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API: the widget's own cross-origin surface (/session, /chat,
# /conversations, /escalate) -- CORS for THESE routes is the widget's
# existing dynamic per-site-key mechanism (Task 1.4.g), already built in
# app/, not Caddy's job.
{$API_HOSTNAME} {
	import security_headers
	# No HTML is ever served from this origin (JSON/SSE only) -- the bare
	# frame-ancestors policy security_headers used to carry is still the
	# right one, just set directly now (see that snippet's own updated
	# header comment for why).
	header Content-Security-Policy "frame-ancestors 'none'"

	request_body {
		max_size {$UPLOAD_MAX_SIZE_BYTES:20971520}
	}

	# docs/SPEC.md §14: "buffering off for /api/v1/chat, long idle
	# timeouts" -- named explicitly because this is the one streaming
	# (SSE) response in the whole API; every other route keeps Caddy's
	# ordinary proxy defaults (handle block below). flush_interval -1
	# disables Caddy's own response buffering entirely (flushes every
	# write immediately); the transport's read/write timeouts are left
	# unbounded (0) rather than guessing a number -- the real ceiling for
	# one turn is already enforced server-side (Settings.chat_request_
	# timeout_seconds, 30s) and by the turn-lease margin on top of it
	# (Settings.chat_lease_margin_seconds); Caddy does not need a second,
	# independently-guessed timeout racing that one.
	@chat path /api/v1/chat
	handle @chat {
		reverse_proxy api:8000 {
			flush_interval -1
			transport http {
				dial_timeout 10s
				read_timeout 0
				write_timeout 0
			}
		}
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy api:8000
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Widget: static assets only, no backend proxying, no cookies, no CORS
# concerns (a <script src> load is never subject to CORS). Served flat
# from one directory -- the loader resolves "chat.js"/"widget.css" as
# SIBLINGS of wherever widget.js itself was loaded from (widget/loader/
# src/config.js's own resolveSiblingUrl()), and chat.js resolves
# "chat.css" the same way relative to its own <script> src (widget/chat/
# src/index.js) -- so all four build outputs (widget/dist/widget.{js,css},
# widget/chat/dist/chat.{js,css}) are copied flat into ONE directory at
# deploy time (docs/deploy/RUNBOOK.md), never nested.
{$WIDGET_HOSTNAME} {
	import security_headers
	# Same reasoning as the api site block above -- static JS/CSS only,
	# the bare frame-ancestors policy, set directly.
	header Content-Security-Policy "frame-ancestors 'none'"

	root * /srv/widget
	# Moderate, short-lived caching, not a long immutable one: unlike the
	# dashboard's Vite output above, these filenames are NOT content-
	# hashed (confirmed by reading widget/scripts/build.mjs -- plain
	# widget.js/widget.css/chat.js/chat.css every build), so a stale
	# browser cache after a redeploy could serve old code under the same
	# URL. 5 minutes bounds that staleness window to something reasonable
	# while still meaningfully reducing load from the same visitor
	# loading the same tenant site repeatedly; must-revalidate makes a
	# cache that outlives max-age re-check with the server (a cheap
	# conditional GET) rather than silently serving stale content past it.
	@assets path *.js *.css
	header @assets Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"
	file_server
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# [SECURITY] L1 PART C (#7) (out-of-band Phase 7 batch, 2026-10-17):
# catch-all for any Host NOT matched by one of the three site blocks
# above -- a real gap found LIVE, not assumed: with no site block of its
# own, a request whose Host matches none of the three above does NOT get
# rejected by Caddy on its own; Caddy's own un-matched-route default is
# a bare "200 OK, Content-Length: 0" (confirmed live, `curl -H "Host:
# evil.com"` against this exact Caddyfile before this block existed),
# not a rejection -- which the task's own "anything else is rejected"
# requirement rules out. `abort` closes the connection with NO response
# at all (not even a status code) -- the strongest rejection available,
# leaking nothing about which hostnames this deployment actually serves
# (an error PAGE, or even a distinct status code, could itself be a
# signal an attacker probing Host values could use). Plain `http://`
# (no hostname) matches only the unencrypted port -- the one port this
# gap exists on: an HTTPS request with an unrecognised SNI is already
# rejected by Caddy's own TLS layer before HTTP routing ever runs (no
# certificate exists for a hostname Caddy was never told to manage), so
# no separate catch-all is needed for 443.
http:// {
	abort
}
